1
00:00:00,940 --> 00:00:03,640
A timing report tells you that a path failed.

2
00:00:04,200 --> 00:00:08,840
The harder question is what to change and how
to know that the change actually helped.

3
00:00:09,680 --> 00:00:11,200
TimingLens connects those steps.

4
00:00:11,900 --> 00:00:17,140
Measured timing, source context, a testable
experiment, and the evidence behind the result.

5
00:00:17,980 --> 00:00:21,380
Start with a Vivado project or checkpoint and
the matching source files.

6
00:00:22,120 --> 00:00:25,160
TimingLens uses your installed Vivado to
inspect the design.

7
00:00:25,160 --> 00:00:26,740
Already have the evidence?

8
00:00:27,560 --> 00:00:30,700
Open a saved TimingLens capture or import
timing

9
00:00:30,700 --> 00:00:35,040
reports alongside your Verilog or SystemVerilog
and XDC constraints.

10
00:00:35,820 --> 00:00:41,060
Block design and Tcl files can add context.
Keep the source revision matched to the build.

11
00:00:41,840 --> 00:00:46,800
Inside the tool, measured cells, nets, and
delays become a connected timing graph.

12
00:00:47,580 --> 00:00:49,200
Source information adds context.

13
00:00:49,200 --> 00:00:53,640
Deterministic engineering rules group related
symptoms into investigations,

14
00:00:54,060 --> 00:00:57,820
so a long logic chain and its routing delay can
be examined together.

15
00:00:58,400 --> 00:00:59,880
The analysis runs locally.

16
00:01:00,720 --> 00:01:03,300
Missing evidence stays visible, and source
hints are

17
00:01:03,300 --> 00:01:06,060
qualified instead of treated as proof of
causality.

18
00:01:07,520 --> 00:01:09,740
Let's follow one design through the
application.

19
00:01:10,360 --> 00:01:14,020
It's a 32-bit counter with a 250 MHz target.

20
00:01:14,860 --> 00:01:16,780
Start here gives us two ways in.

21
00:01:16,780 --> 00:01:22,180
We'll inspect the original build first, then
use the guided experiment to try the fix.

22
00:01:23,240 --> 00:01:24,240
Choose design files.

23
00:01:24,960 --> 00:01:26,240
Here are the three inputs.

24
00:01:26,880 --> 00:01:31,760
The saved timing capture, the SystemVerilog
that produced it, and the clock constraints.

25
00:01:32,460 --> 00:01:36,180
Take a look at the selection, then click
Analyze selected files.

26
00:01:36,900 --> 00:01:40,160
The overview shows 32 failing setup endpoints.

27
00:01:40,840 --> 00:01:44,780
Worst setup slack is minus 0.633 nanoseconds.

28
00:01:44,780 --> 00:01:47,240
Click Review first issue.

29
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:51,800
TimingLens groups the routing delay and the
carry chain on the same paths.

30
00:01:52,340 --> 00:01:56,660
The first recommendation is to shorten the
comparison that drives the counter’s enable.

31
00:01:57,360 --> 00:02:00,700
Change preview shows the expression the tool
wants us to review.

32
00:02:01,260 --> 00:02:03,000
Let's see why it picked that expression.

33
00:02:03,660 --> 00:02:04,700
Open timing path.

34
00:02:05,540 --> 00:02:07,620
Follow the worst path from a counter register

35
00:02:07,620 --> 00:02:10,900
through four carry blocks to a register's clock
enable.

36
00:02:10,900 --> 00:02:14,920
The raw Vivado path gives the deadline and the
arrival time.

37
00:02:15,440 --> 00:02:18,580
The enable is 633 picoseconds late.

38
00:02:19,260 --> 00:02:21,660
Open correlated RTL to see the counter.

39
00:02:22,240 --> 00:02:24,220
The source link identifies the module.

40
00:02:24,820 --> 00:02:28,380
The line to investigate is the condition just
before the subtraction.

41
00:02:28,900 --> 00:02:33,020
On an accepted request, the counter subtracts
only when enough remains.

42
00:02:33,660 --> 00:02:38,740
The original guard compares the entire 32-bit
count against a 4-bit request.

43
00:02:38,740 --> 00:02:41,600
4 bits can ask for at most 15.

44
00:02:42,180 --> 00:02:43,180
That's the opportunity.

45
00:02:44,080 --> 00:02:45,540
Split the count at bit 4.

46
00:02:45,940 --> 00:02:49,520
If any upper bit is set, there are at least 16
left.

47
00:02:50,100 --> 00:02:51,920
Even a request for 15 fits.

48
00:02:52,520 --> 00:02:57,060
If all the upper bits are 0, compare just the
low 4 bits with the request.

49
00:02:57,620 --> 00:02:59,420
9 left and 12 requested?

50
00:02:59,900 --> 00:03:00,580
Hold the count.

51
00:03:01,080 --> 00:03:02,980
The two checks are joined by an OR.

52
00:03:03,460 --> 00:03:07,700
The subtraction stays exactly where it was,
with no extra clock cycle.

53
00:03:07,700 --> 00:03:11,720
Back at Start here, choose Review counter
experiment.

54
00:03:12,300 --> 00:03:16,800
The highlighted diff shows the original guard
in red and its replacement in green.

55
00:03:17,460 --> 00:03:19,060
Open Validation and recipe.

56
00:03:19,740 --> 00:03:24,900
Both versions get the same 4-nanosecond clock
and the same Default implementation recipe.

57
00:03:25,380 --> 00:03:26,660
Only the guard changes.

58
00:03:27,500 --> 00:03:29,320
Select Validate and run experiment.

59
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:34,420
TimingLens first checks the original and the
candidate against an independent model

60
00:03:34,420 --> 00:03:37,980
over 10,289 simulation cycles.

61
00:03:38,500 --> 00:03:43,420
Then Vivado routes the original in its own
directory, followed by the candidate in
another.

62
00:03:43,960 --> 00:03:45,560
We're speeding up the tool runs here.

63
00:03:46,020 --> 00:03:48,080
The status tells you which version is building.

64
00:03:48,680 --> 00:03:52,920
When the builds finish, the whole-core
comparison opens in the Experiment Lab.

65
00:03:53,700 --> 00:03:54,880
Start with setup slack.

66
00:03:55,620 --> 00:04:01,420
Minus 0.633 becomes plus 0.407 nanoseconds.

67
00:04:01,960 --> 00:04:03,540
Next, look at the endpoints.

68
00:04:03,540 --> 00:04:06,640
All 32 setup failures are gone.

69
00:04:07,320 --> 00:04:10,180
Physical lookup tables drop from 50 to 41.

70
00:04:11,040 --> 00:04:12,540
Hold and pulse width pass too.

71
00:04:13,000 --> 00:04:14,520
Open Checks and context.

72
00:04:15,040 --> 00:04:18,460
The standalone core meets timing at 250 MHz.

73
00:04:19,220 --> 00:04:21,980
Timing in the integrated design is still
untested.

74
00:04:22,640 --> 00:04:28,340
Export comparison saves both run records, the
measured differences, and an HTML report.

75
00:04:28,860 --> 00:04:32,420
Another engineer can reopen that evidence and
review the same decision.
